<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Camperdown</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Camperdown was fought on 11 October 1797 off the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> coast near Camperduin between the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> Fleet under Admiral Adam Duncan and the fleet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a> under Vice-Admiral Jan de Winter. The British captured eleven Dutch ships, including de Winter's flagship, ending the Dutch navy as a serious threat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-camperdown.jpg" alt="Battle of Camperdown" width="320" height="207" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Whitcombe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas-Whitcombe-Battle-of-Camperdown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In the winter of 1794–1795 the French Republic overran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> and reorganised it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a>, which gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fleet anchored off the Texel. Britain answered with a force of small, old ships based at Yarmouth under the 65-year-old Admiral Adam Duncan. In the spring of 1797 the Spithead and Nore mutinies paralysed the British fleets, and at one point Duncan was left with only his own Venerable and the Adamant to contain the whole Dutch fleet.</p><p>By September the mutinies were over and Duncan's fleet, which had grown to 17 ships of the line, was blockading the Texel. De Winter had abandoned a plan to join the French at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a> for an invasion of Ireland. When the British returned to Yarmouth for supplies in October, he led his fleet into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>, and on its return to the coast Duncan intercepted it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>De Winter formed a line of battle close to the shore. Fearing that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> would reach the shallows, Duncan abandoned his earlier signals and ordered each ship to steer for and engage her opponent; in poor visibility his line broke into two loose groups. At 12:05 he signalled close action, and soon afterwards Onslow's flagship Monarch broke through the Dutch rear, where superior British numbers overwhelmed the ships of the Dutch rearguard.</p><p>In the north the fight centred on the flagships, Duncan's Venerable engaging de Winter's Vrijheid. Onslow sent his least damaged ships to help, and Powerful and Director joined the attack on Vrijheid at 14:00. As the unengaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ships turned away for the shore, the damaged ones struck their colours one by one, until only the flagship remained in action.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Duncan refused de Winter's sword and shook his hand instead. He did not follow the fleeing ships into the shoals, but took his prizes home; his convoy began to reach Yarmouth on 17 October, and on 20 October he was created Viscount Duncan of Camperdown. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-iii">George III</a> pardoned 180 men condemned for their part in the Nore Mutiny. The losses of the Batavian Navy gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> naval superiority in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> and were a serious blow to French hopes of invading Ireland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Camperdown” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The fleet was destroyed at <b>Camperdown</b> in 1797, colonies were lost to Britain, and trade suffered.</blockquote><a href="/state/batavian-republic">Batavian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
